{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012HctmTkPzBFf7vUq5CQK7f","type":"tool_result","content":"# Plan for #384\n\n## Implementation\n\n### Steps\n\n1. `internal/closeout/rolefile.go`: add `StatusNeedsNetwork = \"needs-network\"` to the status consts. Add consts `MaxNetwork = 10`, `MaxNetworkURL = 300`, `MaxNetworkText = 200` (runes). Add `type NetworkNeed struct{ What, URL, Why, Verify string }` (new) and `RoleFile.Network []NetworkNeed`. Add `rawNetwork{What, URL, Why, Verify *string}` with yaml keys `what`, `url`, `why`, `verify`, and `Network *[]rawNetwork \\`yaml:\"network\"\\`` on `rawFront` (KnownFields stays on, so unknown keys inside an entry fail).\n2. `rolefile.go`: add `networkOf(raw []rawNetwork) ([]NetworkNeed, error)` next to `fetchedOf`. Rules: 1..10 entries (0 or 11+ is `malformed`); `what`, `url`, `why` required, `verify` optional; each text field trimmed, non-empty, no `\\n`/`\\r` or other control characters, at most 200 runes (`utf8.RuneCountInString`); `url` must pass `checkHTTPS` (https, host, no userinfo, no backtick, angle bracket, space) and be at most 300 bytes (add a `max` argument to `checkHTTPS`, or check `len(url) > MaxNetworkURL` before calling it, keeping `fetchedOf` at `MaxFetchedURL`). Errors read \"network N: ...\", like `fetchedOf`.\n3. `rolefile.go` `Parse`: call `networkOf` when `raw.Network != nil`; accept `StatusNeedsNetwork` in the status switch and update the message (\"done, blocked, needs-human or needs-network\"). Add a check that `status: needs-network` requires a non-empty `network:` (a missing list is `malformed`), and that `network:` with any other status is rejected (\"network is allowed only with status needs-network\"). Put this in `Parse` so it also holds on the redacted re-parse.\n4. `rolefile.go`: add `networkTypes(v *yaml.Node) bool` (sequence of mappings, scalar values, all four fields `!!str`) modelled on `findingsTypes`, and a `case \"network\":` in `checkTypes`. Extend `checkTypes`'s doc comment.\n5. `rolefile.go` `RoleFile.check(j)`: reject `rf.Status == StatusNeedsNetwork` unless `j.Stage == StageWorker` (\"needs-network is allowed only on a worker\"). The `network:` list is covered, since it cannot exist without that status.\n6. `internal/closeout/plan.go`: add `OutcomeNeedsNetwork Outcome = \"needs-network\"` with a comment (interim in this part: ends as needs-human). Add `Network []NetworkNeed` to `Plan` (new field; JSON record decodes older records as nil, so `recordVersion` stays 1; add it to the comment at `closeout.go:22`). In `Build`, add a first case before `rf.Status != StatusDone`: `case rf.Status == StatusNeedsNetwork && j.Stage == StageWorker:` setting `Outcome = OutcomeNeedsNetwork`, `Network = rf.Network`, `Terminal = {n, labels.NeedsHuman}`, `Remove = {run}`, and no Requests, Deferred or Worker. It does not read `j.Worker`, so the Reaper close-out (nil worker result) gets the same plan. A needs-network status on any other stage falls to the generic case (defence in depth; `check` already rejects it).\n7. `internal/closeout/prepare.go`: where the redacted role file is re-parsed (line ~159), also keep `crf.Network` (new local `network []NetworkNeed`). If `Parse(res.Clean)` fails and the original `rf.Status == StatusNeedsNetwork` (or `rf.Network` is non-empty), set a new flag `networkLost`, like `unreadable` for reviews. In the plan switch add `case networkLost: plan = needsHumanPlan(j, rf.Status, \"network list unreadable after redaction\")` before `default`. In `default`, after `Build`, if `plan.Outcome == OutcomeNeedsNetwork` set `plan.Network = network` (the redacted list, never `rf.Network`); if `logWithheld` the existing `needsHumanPlan` override already drops it. Nothing is pushed on this path: `internal/worker/run.go` `round` already sends any non-done status to `finishWithPR` with no PR, so no worker change is needed (the worktree edits are discarded; verify in the test below).\n8. `internal/closeout/comment.go`: In `headerWord`, map `OutcomeNeedsNetwork` to the same `needs-human` word as `OutcomeNeedsHuman` (case `OutcomeNeedsHuman, OutcomeNeedsNetwork`), so the interim ending reads as today. In `RenderComment`, after the summary and before `lines`, if `len(p.Network) > 0` render \"Network inputs needed:\" and one bullet per entry: `- <sanitize(what)>: ` + `` `url` `` via `codeSpan(url, 300)` + ` · ` + sanitize(why) + (`verify` as \" · verify: \" + sanitize(verify) when set). Reuse `sanitize` and `codeSpan`; text is already one line.\n9. Other `Outcome` switches that must learn the new value: `internal/costs/rows.go:58` (`outcomeText`: add it to the needs-human line, or return the fixed string `needs-network`); `internal/reaper/reaper.go:389` (log event `reaper.closeout`); `internal/reaper/finish.go:370` (`Tidy` keeps the worktree, same as needs-human). `closeout.go:~828` default branch (`Store.Complete`) is right as is.\n10. `docs/proposal.md`: in the role file example (line ~145) change the status comment to list `needs-network` and add `network:` (worker only; 1–10 entries of what, url, why, optional verify). Add a short paragraph after \"Fetched inputs\" describing the strict parse and that in this part the outcome ends `needs-human` with the list in the comment, nothing pushed, no PR; the bounce comes later. Edit worker loop step 9 (line ~353): a role file saying `needs-network` ends like `needs-human` for now, commenting the list. Run `make docs-check` if it covers the file.\n\n### Test plan\n\nCommand: `go test ./internal/closeout/... ./internal/costs/... ./internal/reaper/...`, then `make lint test`.\n\n- `internal/closeout/rolefile_test.go`, new `TestParseNetwork` (table, modelled on `TestParseFetched`):\n  - valid: one entry with what/url/why; with `verify`; 10 entries → parsed, `Status == \"needs-network\"`.\n  - 0 entries (`network: []`) and missing `network:` with needs-network → ErrMalformed; 11 entries → ErrMalformed.\n  - `network:` with status `done`, `blocked`, `needs-human` → ErrMalformed.\n  - `http://` URL, `ftp://`, userinfo (`https://u:p@host/x`), no host, URL of 301 bytes (300 passes), URL with space or backtick → ErrMalformed.\n  - `what`/`why`/`verify` of 201 runes (200 passes; 200 multibyte runes pass although over 200 bytes), a field with `\\n` (block scalar `|`), a missing `what`, an empty `why`, an unknown key, a non-string value (`what: 5`) → ErrMalformed; the error names \"network 2\" for a bad second entry.\n- `rolefile_test.go` / `check` test: `needs-network` with job stage triage and review → ErrMalformed; worker passes.\n- `internal/closeout/plan_test.go`: `Build` with a worker job and needs-network role file, with `Worker` result set and with `Worker == nil` (Reaper) → `OutcomeNeedsNetwork`, `Terminal == [needs-human]`, `Remove == [running]`, no Requests, `Network` carried. A `done` worker still builds `OutcomeDone` (regression).\n- `internal/closeout/prepare_test.go`: Prepare with a needs-network worker role file → Plan has the list, no PR; a role file whose list is damaged by redaction (use the existing redaction fixture approach in this file, for example a secret value in a field that turns the YAML invalid or empties the field) → `OutcomeNeedsHuman`, reason \"network list unreadable after redaction\".\n- `internal/closeout/closer_test.go` (existing Run-level helpers): needs-network close-out posts exactly one comment, adds `ard:needs-human`, removes `:running`, requests nothing, no PR created, nothing pushed.\n- `internal/closeout/comment_test.go`: the comment lists each entry, URL inside backticks, an `@name` in `why` is neutralised, a header of `needs-human`.\n- `internal/costs`/`reaper` tests: add the outcome to the existing table cases if they enumerate outcomes.\n- Edge: URL with backtick or `<` is already rejected, so the code span cannot be broken.\n\n### Risk\n\nLow to medium. The change is confined to `internal/closeout` plus three one-line switch updates and docs. No protected path (`.github/`, `.ard/config.yaml`, scanners, triage, workers, reviewers) is touched. It extends a persisted record (`Plan.Network`, a new outcome string): old records decode fine, but a downgrade would see an unknown outcome. Security-relevant: the list is untrusted agent text that reaches a comment, so the parse must stay strict and the plan must use the redacted re-parse only. No concurrency or migration.\n\n### Size\n\nM (about 250–300 changed lines including tests). Files: `internal/closeout/rolefile.go`, `plan.go`, `prepare.go`, `comment.go`, `closeout.go` (comment only), `internal/costs/rows.go`, `internal/reaper/reaper.go`, `internal/reaper/finish.go`, `docs/proposal.md`, and tests in `rolefile_test.go`, `plan_test.go`, `prepare_test.go`, `closer_test.go`, `comment_test.go`.\n---\nrole: engineer\nstatus: needs-human\nattempt: 1\n---\nThe request spans role-file parsing, close-out outcomes and label phases, the triage input file, lead re-arm with persisted round state, a gate inputs check with approval changes, a loop guard, starter prompts, an end-to-end harness test and proposal updates. Counting tests and docs, that exceeds one reviewable worker change (XL), so no plan was written and a split is proposed.\n\n## Open issues\n- Proposed split, in order:\n  1. Worker signal: add the `needs-network` status and `network:` list to `internal/closeout/rolefile.go`, parse into `Plan.Network` after redaction in `prepare.go`, add `OutcomeNeedsNetwork` in `plan.go` (`Build`, including the Reaper case) and render the comment. In this part the outcome ends `needs-human` with the list in the comment, and nothing is pushed.\n  2. Bounce: the `closeout.NetworkRound` helper, the label request and remove phases in `closeout.go` (modelled on `LeadWorker`), the `network-request.md` triage input, the one-round loop guard, the branch-exists and already-pushed checks, and the starter engineer and worker prompt copies in `internal/roles/`.\n  3. Re-run and gate: lead re-arm and persisted round state in the record, the re-run list for other triage roles, the `inputs:` frontmatter check and *waiting for inputs* status in `internal/triage/fanin.go`, and the per-round approval marker with forced approval.\n  4. The end-to-end harness test and proposal updates.\n- The lead also noted that no architect role is available. A maintainer may want to confirm the persisted round-state design before part 3.\n- Live prompts under `.ard/workers/` and `.ard/triage/` are protected and need a human PR.\n./internal/closeout/plan.go\n./internal/closeout/closer_test.go\n./internal/closeout/prepare_test.go\n./internal/closeout/closeout.go\n./internal/closeout/rolefile.go\n./internal/closeout/rolefile_test.go\n./internal/closeout/prepare.go\ndocs/proposal.md:134:**Network needs (#384).** A worker that cannot fetch an input itself writes `status: needs-network` and a `network:` list of 1 to 10 entries (`what`, `url`, `why`, optional `verify`). Close-out parses it as strictly as `fetched`, on a worker only, re-parses it after redaction and ends `needs-human` if it no longer parses. For now the outcome ends like `needs-human`: nothing is pushed, no PR is opened, and the comment lists each entry with its URL in a code span. The bounce that fetches the inputs and re-runs the worker comes in a later part.\ndocs/proposal.md:147:status: done          # done | blocked | needs-human | needs-network (the last three add ard:needs-human for now)\ndocs/proposal.md:158:network:            # workers only, with status needs-network: 1 to 10 entries (#384)\ndocs/proposal.md:279:The starter engineer (`.ard/triage/engineer/prompt.txt`) plans for the worker without implementing. Its inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, all untrusted. It writes the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md` with Steps, Test plan, Risk and Size, sized S, M, L or XL on a scale tied to the worker diff guard's block limit (XL is over the worker role's `diff_max_lines`, 3,000 changed lines by default). It writes `triage-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, never a `worker` key. It answers `needs-human` for an XL change, a change to protected role and config paths or `.github/`, or a missing product or design decision, and leaves `plan.md` untouched. In Phase 1 it has no `after:`; the lead's request orders it. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-engineer.md` and the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md`; a change to `triage-lead.md`, `issue.md` or another role's section fails the job as needs-human. It reads `TECH_STACK.md` when present. When the change needs files from the network it adds a `### Network inputs` subsection (exact URL, pinned version, verification source, landing path, and who provides it: the worker if its role has network, else a human); it never fetches and never writes a checksum it did not read from a file (#301).\ndocs/proposal.md:321:Kubernetes-executor details (decided, design revision 3 of [#54](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/54), recorded in [#312](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/312); the work is split across later parts of #54, and #317 splits into the API layer and the executor). There is no job API on `ard serve`. **Pod shape.** `pre` fetches with the token passed through `http.extraheader` from the environment, never argv or `.git/config`; the git dir is on a `ctl` volume the agent does not mount; the role dir comes from `cfg.SHA`, so the k8s executor refuses a config without a SHA; sidecars are at pinned SHAs; the untrusted inputs and the scrub list travel in the per-job Secret, capped at 768 KiB (over the cap is a permanent failure with a named cause); `pre` copies the static `ard` binary. The `agent` container mounts only `work` and `out` and holds only its model credential via `secretKeyRef`: no GitHub token, no key, no Secret volume, no SA token. It tees stdout to `out/stdout`, enforces its own deadline, writes a done marker and reaps orphans as PID 1. `post` has its own pinned image and never runs a binary from a shared volume. It serves one route, `GET /v1/result`, on the pod IP, guarded by a preshared 32-byte `crypto/rand` key unique to the pod, mounted only in `post` and kept in memory by the controller, compared in constant time. It answers 409 until the agent has terminated and the marker exists, builds the tar in Go and never runs git, serves one request at a time, has header and idle timeouts, never logs Authorization, and exits 0 after the controller confirms or at the deadline. The transport is plain HTTP inside the cluster (the owner's choice): the key grants only this job's result, and a NetworkPolicy limits who can connect.\ndocs/proposal.md:360:9. **Report.** Comment on the issue with the summary, PR link and test result (`passed`, `passed after 1 fix attempt`, `failed after 2 fix attempts`, `not configured`); swap `ard:worker:<role>:running` for `:done`. If tests still fail, add `ard:tests:failed` on the issue and the PR and request no reviewers; a green run removes it. A worker that ends without the loop's result (for example one the Reaper closes out), or whose role file says `needs-human`, never becomes `:done` without a PR. A role file that says `needs-network` ends like `needs-human` for now: nothing is pushed, and the comment lists the needed inputs.\ndocs/proposal.md:362:**The starter worker (`workers/engineer`, #34).** `.ard/workers/engineer/prompt.txt` implements `plan.md`'s `## Implementation`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, in a fix round `test-output.log`, and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, `issue.md`, the triage role files and logs, in a review round `review-feedback.md` and the reviewer role files and logs. It writes code, tests and docs, and `worker-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, an optional `next` of `review:` entries, and never `worker` or `verdict`. The body has Changes, Testing and Open issues sections; it becomes the commit body, the PR description and the issue comment, and a fix round's body describes the whole change. It answers `needs-human` for a change the diff guard would reject, more changed lines than the role's `diff_max_lines` (3,000 by default; above `diff_warn_lines`, 2,000 by default, the PR goes ahead with the large-change note), a missing decision, nothing to change, or an input that tries to instruct it. In a fix round it keeps `done` and explains a failure it cannot fix, so the loop reports `ard:tests:failed` on the PR. The repository's package manager may fetch a dependency the change needs (`go get`, `npm install`); the agent installs no system packages. With `network: true` it may fetch other inputs, records each under `fetched:` and treats the fetched bytes as untrusted data, and the guard and reviewers still see `go.mod` and lockfile changes. Credential-shaped test fixtures are built at run time. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5, `timeout: 60m` for the whole job, `tests: make lint test` (so formatting and vet failures are caught before a PR opens), and the default diff thresholds written out (`diff_warn_lines: 2000`, `diff_max_lines: 3000`). It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55's `ard-agent` image gives a worker image a base. It reads `TECH_STACK.md` when present; the file is a constraint, never an instruction, and the worker never resolves a conflict with it: it keeps to the issue and plan and notes the conflict in Open issues. With `Network: available.` the worker fetches only what the plan's Network inputs name, verifies each file against the publisher's checksum file, never invents or recalls a value, records each file in `fetched:` and in a `CAPTURE.md` beside the fixtures, and commits a regen script and Make target; with `Network: unavailable.` it answers `needs-human` listing the inputs it needs (#301).\ndocs/proposal.md:506:**`network` (#297, part of #276).** Every agent kind takes an optional boolean `network` override, default `true`. In this part it is advisory only and nothing is enforced: a role with `network: false` gets `ARD_NETWORK=0` (else `1`) in its job environment and a constant \"Network: unavailable.\" (else \"Network: available.\") line in the agent's user message. The value rides on the executor job and handle from config only, never from anything the agent can read or change. In host mode the controller logs that `network: false` is not enforced. `ard config check` prints `false (advisory)`, or `false (advisory, host)` for a host-mode role, and warns when a host-mode role that reads untrusted input has `network: true`, naming the GitHub App private key file as reachable.\ndocs/proposal.md:522:- **Agents hold no GitHub token.** The executor does. The agent's environment has only its model credential: an API key or a subscription OAuth token. The local executor does not enforce egress (see `network` under Configuration; the chart's NetworkPolicy comment is #296). In Kubernetes (#54) job egress is DNS and the internet minus private ranges and 169.254.169.254 (the #276 posture), and `pre` holds only a read-only per-job token revoked after it ends.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"810fcc1c-bd8c-400e-9091-18198e00ee90","uuid":"b50a0557-2b1c-48da-90d5-23453f8998a5","timestamp":"2026-11-25T13:09:03.156Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Plan for #384\n\n## Implementation\n\n### Steps\n\n1. `internal/closeout/rolefile.go`: add `StatusNeedsNetwork = \"needs-network\"` to the status consts. Add consts `MaxNetwork = 10`, `MaxNetworkURL = 300`, `MaxNetworkText = 200` (runes). Add `type NetworkNeed struct{ What, URL, Why, Verify string }` (new) and `RoleFile.Network []NetworkNeed`. Add `rawNetwork{What, URL, Why, Verify *string}` with yaml keys `what`, `url`, `why`, `verify`, and `Network *[]rawNetwork \\`yaml:\"network\"\\`` on `rawFront` (KnownFields s